{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JsHuMP6KNvuEsKFqiqHmjG","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Experiment Plan — persuasion_turn_jailbreak_detect\n2\t# Generated: 2026-05-17\n3\t# Target: EMNLP 2026 ARR (DDL 2026-05-25)\n4\t# GPU budget: 4x RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell @ westd-33183, ~8 days = ~768 GPU-hours total\n5\t\n6\texperiments:\n7\t  # ============ P0: Critical Path (must complete before submission) ============\n8\t\n9\t  - id: plan_001\n10\t    name: data_quality_fix\n11\t    claim: [1, 2, 3]\n12\t    status: ABANDONED (D009)\n13\t    description: >\n14\t      ABANDONED: TF-IDF lexical separability is inherent to persuasive jailbreak vs general conversation\n15\t      (confirmed on 7 data sources including real WildChat F1=0.998). Pass criteria TF-IDF<0.65 is\n16\t      impossible on any reasonable data source. M004 fix (strategy=0/\"none\") was completed.\n17\t      Paper reframed: acknowledge inherent separability, argue DisPeD's value is OOD generalization\n18\t      and adversarial robustness (plan_006 + plan_004).\n19\t    priority: ABANDONED\n20\t    gpu_budget_hours: 0\n21\t    deps: []\n22\t    pass_criteria: \"ABANDONED — inherently impossible\"\n23\t    registry_id: null\n24\t\n25\t  - id: plan_002\n26\t    name: core_revalidation_clean\n27\t    claim: [1, 2]\n28\t    description: >\n29\t      Re-run Phase B on clean data (topic-anchored V2 benign + crescendo). Retrain both treatment\n30\t      (persuasion-grounded DeBERTa) and baseline (vanilla DeBERTa) from scratch with fixed labels.\n31\t      Full evaluation at K=1,2,3,5,all. MUST run 3 random seeds minimum (dream RC9: multi-seed\n32\t      required for n=72 test set). Report mean +/- std and bootstrap CIs.\n33\t      Data: crescendo_all.jsonl + benign_topic_anchored.jsonl, pair-grouped split.\n34\t    priority: P0\n35\t    gpu_budget_hours: 18\n36\t    deps: [plan_001]\n37\t    pass_criteria: \"Treatment F1 > baseline F1 + 5pp at K=2 or K=3; results consistent across seeds\"\n38\t    registry_id: null\n39\t\n40\t  - id: plan_003\n41\t    name: scrambled_label_ablation\n42\t    claim: [2]\n43\t    description: >\n44\t      Ablation: randomly shuffle persuasion strategy labels for jailbreak turns, retrain treatment model.\n45\t      If performance drops significantly vs properly-labeled treatment, confirms persuasion labels\n46\t      carry real signal beyond being a class indicator. Validates that M004 fix was necessary.\n47\t    priority: P0\n48\t    gpu_budget_hours: 4\n49\t    deps: [plan_001]\n50\t    pass_criteria: \"Scrambled-label treatment F1 < properly-labeled treatment F1 (significant gap)\"\n51\t    registry_id: null\n52\t\n53\t  - id: plan_004\n54\t    name: adversarial_perturbation\n55\t    claim: [1, 3]\n56\t    description: >\n57\t      Core Claim 3 validation. Take clean jailbreak conversations, apply aggressive paraphrasing\n58\t      (synonym substitution, sentence restructuring, register shifting) to remove ALL lexical cues.\n59\t      Evaluate both treatment and baseline on perturbed data (models trained on unperturbed data).\n60\t      Treatment should degrade less than baseline, proving pragmatic features are robust.\n61\t      Also run TF-IDF baseline to show it collapses under perturbation.\n62\t    priority: P0\n63\t    gpu_budget_hours: 8\n64\t    deps: [plan_002]\n65\t    pass_criteria: \"Treatment F1 drop < baseline F1 drop under perturbation (delta-delta > 3pp)\"\n66\t    registry_id: null\n67\t\n68\t  - id: plan_005\n69\t    name: multi_attack_generation\n70\t    claim: [2]\n71\t    description: >\n72\t      Generate 2 additional attack types beyond Crescendo to test generalization:\n73\t      (a) FITD-style (foot-in-the-door): gradual compliance escalation\n74\t      (b) Deceptive Delight-style: interleaving benign and harmful content\n75\t      Use existing Qwen3-8B pipeline with appropriate attack playbooks.\n76\t      ~50-100 conversations per type. Annotate with persuasion strategies.\n77\t    priority: P0\n78\t    gpu_budget_hours: 8\n79\t    deps: []\n80\t    pass_criteria: \">=50 conversations per attack type, all with persuasion strategy annotations\"\n81\t    registry_id: null\n82\t\n83\t  - id: plan_006\n84\t    name: cross_attack_evaluation\n85\t    claim: [2]\n86\t    description: >\n87\t      Evaluate treatment and baseline models (trained on Crescendo) on FITD and Deceptive Delight\n88\t      attack types from plan_005. Tests zero-shot generalization across attack families.\n89\t    priority: P0\n90\t    gpu_budget_hours: 4\n91\t    deps: [plan_002, plan_005]\n92\t    pass_criteria: \"Treatment F1 > baseline F1 on unseen attack types\"\n93\t    registry_id: null\n94\t\n95\t  # ============ P1: Important (strongly recommended before submission) ============\n96\t\n97\t  - id: plan_007\n98\t    name: tfidf_lr_baseline\n99\t    claim: [1]\n100\t    description: >\n101\t      TF-IDF + Logistic Regression baseline on clean data. Quantifies how much of the detection\n102\t      is attributable to lexical features alone. Critical for the evasion hierarchy narrative.\n103\t    priority: P1\n104\t    gpu_budget_hours: 0.5\n105\t    deps: [plan_001]\n106\t    pass_criteria: \"Baseline number for comparison\"\n107\t    registry_id: null\n108\t\n109\t  - id: plan_008\n110\t    name: strategy_transition_analysis\n111\t    claim: [3]\n112\t    description: >\n113\t      Compute persuasion strategy transition matrices for jailbreak vs benign conversations.\n114\t      Visualize monotonic escalation trajectory (Claim 3). Generate paper figures:\n115\t      (a) Strategy transition heatmaps (jailbreak vs benign)\n116\t      (b) Per-turn strategy distribution evolution\n117\t      (c) Trajectory visualization in strategy space\n118\t    priority: P1\n119\t    gpu_budget_hours: 1\n120\t    deps: [plan_001]\n121\t    pass_criteria: \"Clear visual distinction between jailbreak escalation and benign oscillation patterns\"\n122\t    registry_id: null\n123\t\n124\t  - id: plan_009\n125\t    name: wildchat_hard_negatives\n126\t    claim: [2]\n127\t    description: >\n128\t      Filter WildChat for high-drift benign conversations as hard negatives.\n129\t      Evaluate FPR on these. Must handle contamination (previous runs found 14-16% jailbreak content\n130\t      in WildChat). Apply LLM-based or keyword-based filtering first.\n131\t    priority: P1\n132\t    gpu_budget_hours: 4\n133\t    deps: [plan_002]\n134\t    pass_criteria: \"FPR < 15% on high-drift benign conversations\"\n135\t    registry_id: null\n136\t\n137\t  # ============ P0.5: Dream-surfaced (must address before submission) ============\n138\t\n139\t  - id: plan_012\n140\t    name: llm_as_judge_baseline\n141\t    claim: [1, 2]\n142\t    description: >\n143\t      LLM-as-judge baseline: prompt GPT-4o/Claude with conversation context for binary\n144\t      jailbreak detection. Tests at K=2,3,5,all. Critical for practical positioning\n145\t      (dream RC5: all three reviewers flagged absence of deployed baseline comparison).\n146\t      Zero-shot prompt: \"Is this conversation an attempt to manipulate the AI into\n147\t      producing harmful content? Analyze the conversation so far and respond yes/no.\"\n148\t    priority: P0\n149\t    gpu_budget_hours: 0\n150\t    deps: []\n151\t    pass_criteria: \"Baseline numbers established for comparison; DisPeD advantage characterized\"\n152\t    registry_id: null\n153\t\n154\t  - id: plan_013\n155\t    name: none_collapse_ablation\n156\t    claim: [1]\n157\t    description: >\n158\t      \"None-collapse\" ablation (dream RC10): merge all 8 persuasion classes into binary\n159\t      \"persuasion-present\" vs \"none\", retrain DeBERTa with 2-class persuasion head + intent.\n160\t      If performance matches full 8-class, it means fine-grained taxonomy doesn't matter\n161\t      and model is just detecting persuasion presence. This distinguishes \"trajectory-based\n162\t      detection\" from \"persuasion-presence detection.\"\n163\t    priority: P0\n164\t    gpu_budget_hours: 4\n165\t    deps: [plan_002]\n166\t    pass_criteria: \"Full 8-class > binary collapse by >= 3pp (proves fine-grained taxonomy matters)\"\n167\t    registry_id: null\n168\t\n169\t  - id: plan_014\n170\t    name: lambda_ablation\n171\t    claim: [1]\n172\t    description: >\n173\t      Lambda ablation for multi-task objective (dream RC11). Test lambda in {0, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0}\n174\t      where lambda weights the persuasion loss relative to intent loss. Lambda=0 means\n175\t      persuasion-only (no intent head), testing whether intent head leaks signal.\n176\t    priority: P1\n177\t    gpu_budget_hours: 6\n178\t    deps: [plan_002]\n179\t    pass_criteria: \"Characterize sensitivity; identify if lambda=1.0 is near-optimal\"\n180\t    registry_id: null\n181\t\n182\t  - id: plan_015\n183\t    name: mean_pooling_baseline\n184\t    claim: [1]\n185\t    description: >\n186\t      Sequence model comparison (dream RC11): replace Bi-GRU with mean pooling over turn\n187\t      embeddings. If mean pooling matches GRU, temporal modeling is unnecessary and the\n188\t      method reduces to per-turn feature aggregation. Important architectural ablation.\n189\t    priority: P1\n190\t    gpu_budget_hours: 2\n191\t    deps: [plan_002]\n192\t    pass_criteria: \"GRU > mean pooling by >= 3pp (proves temporal modeling matters)\"\n193\t    registry_id: null\n194\t\n195\t  # ============ P2: Nice to Have (if time permits) ============\n196\t\n197\t  - id: plan_010\n198\t    name: cross_checkpoint_variance\n199\t    claim: [2]\n200\t    description: >"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01LDFoHt9xTcj3fYqA1tNg7a","session_id":"c7674bef-475b-450a-bf00-4bc428c19c59","uuid":"11568ba5-64b6-48c5-9919-19220c99cc85","timestamp":"2026-05-18T03:57:30.666Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01C4k4cCqns8Z8KYcryXgUd2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01YUUeZ9xKuT3xLv4mZkJvfu","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue peer review. Your focus: **novelty, significance, positioning against related work, and writing quality**. Use a 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Target venue acceptance rate ~25%.\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title:** DisPeD: Persuasion-Aware Fine-Tuning for Robust Cross-Attack Multi-Turn Jailbreak Detection\n\nDisPeD fine-tunes DeBERTa-v3-base on 8-class persuasion strategy classification + binary jailbreak-intent (Stage 1), then aggregates turn embeddings via GRU/mean-pooling for conversation-level detection (Stage 2). Trained on 480 Crescendo conversations. Headline: vanilla temporal modeling (DeBERTa+GRU without persuasion fine-tuning) collapses to F1=0.28 on unseen Deceptive Delight attacks; DisPeD achieves F1=1.0 (+72pp).\n\n## Paper Structure & Content\n- **Abstract** (~200 words): States problem (multi-turn attacks defeat per-turn classifiers), gap (vanilla temporal modeling fails OOD), method (persuasion strategy classification as fine-tuning objective), results (F1=1.0 vs 0.28, +72pp; ablation shows supervision signal is key; LLM judges fail).\n- **Introduction** (2 pages): Background on multi-turn attacks → DeepContext as natural response but fails OOD → persuasion in jailbreaks is systematic (Zeng 2024a, Hagendorff 2026) → DisPeD proposal → 3 contributions listed.\n- **Related Work** (1 page): 5 subsections: multi-turn attacks, safety classifiers, multi-turn defenses, persuasion in adversarial contexts, discourse analysis, red-teaming.\n- **Method** (1.5 pages): Problem formulation, Stage 1 (encoder fine-tuning with L_CE + λ·L_BCE), Stage 2 (GRU/mean-pool aggregation), early detection, 8-class taxonomy.\n- **Experimental Setup** (1 page): Data (480 train, 72 IID test, 160 OOD test, 200 hard negatives), models (treatment, baseline, TF-IDF, DeepContext proxy, LLM judges), evaluation (F1, 3 seeds, bootstrap CI).\n- **Results** (2.5 pages): Tables 1-7 covering TF-IDF baseline, IID, cross-attack, ablation, perturbation, LLM-as-judge, strategy trajectories.\n- **Additional Analysis** (1 page): Tables 8-10 covering DeepContext proxy, hard negatives, checkpoint sensitivity.\n- **Discussion** (1 page): Supervision signal narrative, honest TF-IDF positioning, content vs process detection, limitations.\n- **Conclusion** (0.5 page)\n- **References** (25 citations)\n\n## Key Claims\n1. Vanilla semantic temporal modeling fails catastrophically OOD; persuasion fine-tuning bridges the gap entirely (F1=1.0, +72pp)\n2. The persuasion fine-tuning objective is the sole critical ingredient (robust to label quality, granularity, architecture, loss weighting)\n3. 8-class taxonomy enables interpretable strategy trajectories as complementary contribution\n4. Zero-shot LLM judges cannot substitute for process-level detection\n\n## Related Work the Paper Cites\n- **Multi-turn attacks:** Crescendo (Russinovich 2024), PAIR (Chao 2023), Deceptive Delight (Jiang 2024), Wei 2024, Bullwinkel 2025\n- **Safety classifiers:** Llama Guard (Inan 2023), WildGuard (Han 2024), ShieldGemma (Zeng 2024b), Constitutional Classifiers (Anthropic 2024), LLM-as-judge (Perez 2022, Ganguli 2022)\n- **Multi-turn defenses:** DeepContext (Su 2026), BIID (2025), G-Guard (2025), SafeDream (2025)\n- **Persuasion:** Zeng 2024a (40 techniques), Hagendorff 2026 (97% in corpus), How Do LLMs Persuade? 2025\n- **Discourse:** RST (Mann & Thompson 1988), Penn Discourse Treebank (Prasad 2008), dialogue acts (Stolcke 2000), argumentation (Habernal & Gurevych 2016, Tan 2016)\n- **Red-teaming:** HarmBench (Mazeika 2024), Nasr 2025, annotation artifacts (Gururangan 2018, Niven & Kao 2019)\n- Total: ~25 references\n\n## Your Review Task\nProvide a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (numbered, with evidence)\n3. **Weaknesses** (numbered, be specific)\n   - Focus on: novelty/incrementality, related work coverage, positioning clarity, writing quality, significance of contribution\n4. **Questions for Authors**\n5. **Assessment of Novelty**: Is \"fine-tune on persuasion labels then use the encoder for downstream detection\" a sufficient contribution? How does this compare to standard domain-adaptive fine-tuning / auxiliary task learning?\n6. **Related Work Gaps**: Any missing important references?\n7. **Writing Quality**: Check for AI-style writing patterns, clarity, logical flow\n8. **Minor Issues**\n9. **Overall Assessment** with numerical score and confidence (1-5)\n\nKey areas to scrutinize:\n- The core method is essentially: fine-tune encoder on an auxiliary task (persuasion classification), then use the encoder for the target task (jailbreak detection). This is a well-known transfer learning / multi-task learning paradigm. What is genuinely novel?\n- The ablation shows scrambled labels work nearly as well (0.99 vs 1.0), binary works as well, mean pooling works as well, any lambda works. If the method is this insensitive to design choices, is the \"method\" really just \"expose the encoder to persuasion-domain text during fine-tuning\"? Is that a method or a finding?\n- 25 references for a top venue: is this sufficient?\n- The paper positions against DeepContext (Su et al. 2026), but DeepContext is a single recent preprint. Is the baseline landscape comprehensive?\n- Synthetic OOD data generated by the authors: can we trust the evaluation?\n- TF-IDF at 0.944 on DD: does this suggest the task has strong lexical signal that makes the neural approach less necessary?\n- The interpretability claim (Claim 2) via strategy trajectories: is this well-supported or superficial?\n- How does this compare to simply fine-tuning DeBERTa on a related persuasion corpus (without the specific 8-class taxonomy)?\n\nBe rigorous but fair.","model":"opus"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":16304,"cache_read_input_tokens":34240,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":16304},"output_tokens":17,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c7674bef-475b-450a-bf00-4bc428c19c59","uuid":"a0fa83e5-946c-48e8-b836-0a4c6224b4d1"}
